#
# build-speed.fragment — OPTIONAL, independent of x453ma.fragment
#
# Apply AFTER x453ma.fragment:
#   scripts/kconfig/merge_config.sh -m -O build build/.config \
#       config/x453ma.fragment config/build-speed.fragment
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scope: this file will NOT turn a multi-hour build into a fast one.
#
# Where build time actually goes on this class of machine (2 cores, 4 GiB,
# SATA SSD, GCC 16):
#
#   ~25-30k object files, compiled from scratch on every clean build. That
#   is effectively the entire cost, and it is dominated by sheer object count.
#   Neither a config option nor a kernel patch changes that.
#
# Ranked by size:
#   1. ccache on rebuilds         HUGE   (hours -> minutes after config tweaks)
#   2. driver/module count        MODERATE (already trimmed by x453ma.fragment)
#   3. -j tuning                  SMALL   (2 real cores, memory-bound link)
#   4. objtool / ORC              SMALL   (~5-10% of build time)
#   5. ftrace instrumentation      SMALL   (also works around a build error)
#   6. the settings in this file  SMALL
#
# ccache is the largest win and is not listed here because it is a toolchain
# setting rather than a kernel option; scripts/x453ma-kernel.sh enables it
# automatically when installed. See README section 8.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#

# 1. ftrace. This one is worth doing for a reason beyond speed.
#
#    It FIXES A BUILD FAILURE. On this tree, x86_64_defconfig yields
#    CONFIG_FTRACE=y with CONFIG_FUNCTION_TRACER unset, and the build dies
#    in `make prepare` with:
#        arch/x86/include/asm/ftrace.h:9: error: #error Compiler does not
#        support fentry?
#    because Makefile only defines CC_USING_FENTRY when its
#    `cc-option-yn -mfentry` probe succeeds. Whether that probe failure is
#    pre-existing or specific to this config was not established.
#
#    Turning ftrace off sidesteps it entirely and also removes the
#    instrumentation and the kprobe/ftrace plumbing from the build. Nothing in
#    a desktop userspace needs function tracing, so this costs no functionality.
#
#    Note this does NOT touch the Spectre/MDS/IBPB CPU mitigations, which are
#    a separate mechanism (CONFIG_MITIGATION_*) and are left untouched on
#    purpose. Do not confuse the two.
# CONFIG_FTRACE is not set
# CONFIG_DYNAMIC_FTRACE is not set
# CONFIG_FUNCTION_TRACER is not set
# CONFIG_KPROBE_EVENTS is not set
# CONFIG_KPROBE_SAMPLE is not set

# 2. Skip objtool on every object file.
#
#    CONFIG_UNWINDER_ORC runs objtool over essentially every .o to insert ORC
#    unwinder records. That is a real, measurable slice of the build.
#    CONFIG_UNWINDER_FRAME_POINTER replaces it with a plain frame-pointer
#    walk: no objtool pass at all.
#
#    TRADE-OFF: frame-pointer unwinding costs a little runtime performance
#    (crash backtraces and perf look slightly different, ~1-3% on some
#    workloads). ORC is better at runtime; this file is about build time.
#    If you would rather keep ORC, delete this stanza — it is the only part
#    here that costs you anything at run time.
# CONFIG_UNWINDER_ORC is not set
# CONFIG_UNWINDER_FRAME_POINTER=y

# 3. Do not keep data symbols for module debugging.
#
#    Smaller kallsyms => a smaller vmlinux and a cheaper link.
#    Costs you: module *data* symbols in /proc/kallsyms, so some crash
#    backtraces through modules get less readable.
# # CONFIG_KALLSYMS_ALL is not set

# 4. Do not die on compiler warnings.
#
#    CONFIG_WERROR=y is in defconfig. GCC 16 is newer than this tree and will
#    eventually emit a warning this kernel treats as an error, which turns a
#    cosmetic warning into a failed build. You have already hit one such
#    toolchain interaction (fentry, above). A warning is not a build failure.
CONFIG_WERROR=n

# 5. Skip deflate work at install time (not build time).
# CONFIG_MODULE_COMPRESS_NONE=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Explicitly NOT changed here, and why — these are common "optimisations" that
# would be mistakes on this machine:
#
#   LTO / CONFIG_LTO_THIN / CONFIG_LTO_FULL
#       This is the single most counterproductive thing you can do here. LTO
#       is the usual first answer to "make the kernel smaller", but it makes
#       the BUILD dramatically slower, and its memory appetite during the link
#       stage will thrash a 4 GiB machine with ~1.9 GiB of swap. It would
#       make your 4-hour problem into an 8-hour problem. Stay on LTO_NONE.
#       (Also: you have no clang installed, so the good fast-LTO path — clang
#       + ThinLTO — is not available to you anyway. GCC LTO is the slow one.)
#
#   CONFIG_CC_OPTIMIZE_FOR_SIZE
#       "-Os" is not reliably faster to compile than -O2 on modern GCC, and it
#       makes the CPU-bound parts of your desktop slower. That is a bad trade
#       for a laptop.
#
#   make localmodconfig
#       Produces a config that depends on what happened to be loaded today.
#       You will lose the SD card reader, the fallback NIC, and anything only
#       needed on a cold path, and it will differ every time you run it.
#       Already rejected in the main README; do not reintroduce it here.
#
#   CONFIG_CC_VERSION_CHECK / custom gcc versions
#       Cosmetic speed claims in old kernel-build guides; no measurable effect.
#
#   Stripping -Wall / -Werror everywhere or disabling objtool globally
#       Hides real bugs. Disabling warnings on a tree you are rebasing onto
#       new compilers is how you end up debugging a silently broken kernel.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------